.TH OTPME-GROUP 1 "2026-10-31" "OTPme 0.3.0a288" "OTPme Manual"
.SH NAME
otpme-group \- manage groups: the POSIX groups of the users, in LDAP and on the hosts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B otpme-group
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIgroup\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.na
.nh
.B otpme-group
adds, changes and deletes groups.
A group is a POSIX group: it has a name and a \fBgidNumber\fR, and in LDAP and on the hosts it lists its members.
.PP
The members are the owners of the tokens put into the group, also of those in its roles, and the users whose default group it is, see \fBToken and Role Assignment\fR.
A user is never put in directly.
Every user has a default group, its primary group on the hosts.
A new user gets the one of \fB\-\-group\fR, else the one of its template, else the one a \fBdefaultgroups\fR policy names, else the group named like the site.
\fBotpme\-user group\fR changes it, see
.BR otpme\-user (1).
.PP
A group has two more uses:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Its hosts are hosts of the shares that are limited to hosts and have the group, see \fBHost Management\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Its sync users are copied to the hosts that name it as sync group, see \fBSync User Management\fR.
.RE
.PP
A new group goes into the unit \fBgroups\fR, unless a \fBdefaultunits\fR policy names another.
The groups the realm and the sites come with (\fBroot\fR, \fBrealmusers\fR and the group named like each site) cannot be deleted, moved or renamed.
.hy
.ad
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Group Management
.na
.nh
.TP
\fBadd\fR [\fB\-\-attributes\fR \fIattr=value,...\fR] \fIgroup\fR
Add a group.
With a path, e.g. \fBlinux/staff\fR, it goes into that unit; else into the unit a \fBdefaultunits\fR policy names, see
.BR otpme\-policy (1),
or into \fBgroups\fR.
The name is unique in the realm, over all sites.
.br
\fB\-\-attributes\fR sets attributes of the posix extension, e.g. \fBgidNumber=5000\fR; one it does not have is refused.
Without \fBgidNumber\fR the group gets the next free one, see \fBidrange\fR in
.BR otpme\-policy (1).
.br
Needs \fBadd:group\fR on the unit.
.TP
\fBdel\fR [\fB\-\-no\-trash\fR] \fIgroup\fR
Delete the group.
It goes to the trash, see
.BR otpme\-trash (1);
\fB\-\-no\-trash\fR deletes it right away.
.br
A group that still has tokens or roles, or is the default group of a user, is not deleted; the refusal names them.
\fB\-f\fR deletes it anyway, and what held it keeps a reference to something that is gone; \fBremove_orphans\fR clears it up.
The groups the realm and the sites come with (\fBroot\fR, \fBrealmusers\fR and the group named like each site) cannot be deleted.
.br
Needs \fBdelete:object\fR on the group or \fBdelete:group\fR on the unit.
.TP
\fBshow\fR [\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-z\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-fields\fR \fIF1,F2\fR] [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-role\-limit\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-token\-limit\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-host\-limit\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-policy\-limit\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-sort\-by\fR \fIfield\fR] [\fB\-\-reverse\fR] [\fB\-\-raw\fR] [\fB\-\-csv\fR] [\fB\-\-csv\-sep\fR \fIsep\fR] \fR[\fIgroup\fR]
Without a group: a table of all of them, with their unit, status, roles, tokens, hosts, sync users, policies, ACL inheritance and description.
.RS
.TP
.B \-a
Also the groups you may only look at, not just the ones you may change.
.TP
.B \-z \fIN\fR
How many groups to print.
.TP
.B \-\-fields \fIF1,F2\fR
Print these columns and no others, in the order they are named.
A name with a \fB+\fR in front is added to the columns that are shown anyway, and one with a \fB\-\fR is left out of them.
.TP
.B \-\-limit \fIN\fR
How many of each kind to print per group: roles, tokens, hosts, sync users, policies.
.TP
.B \-\-role\-limit \fIN\fR, \fB\-\-token\-limit \fIN\fR, \fB\-\-host\-limit \fIN\fR, \fB\-\-policy\-limit \fIN\fR
The same for one of them.
.TP
.B \-\-sort\-by \fIfield\fR, \fB\-\-reverse\fR
Which column to sort by, and the other way round.
.TP
.B \-\-raw\fR, \fB\-\-csv\fR, \fB\-\-csv\-sep\fR \fIsep\fR
\fB\-\-raw\fR leaves the header and the borders out, \fB\-\-csv\fR prints one row per line with \fB;\fR between the columns, and \fB\-\-csv\-sep\fR uses another separator.
.RE
.IP
A column you may not see says \fB\-\fR.
With a group named, this prints the object itself instead of a table row, one value per line.
\fBshow\fR \f(BIgroup\fB$\fR prints that one group as a table row.
A name that is no group is a pattern with \fB*\fR, not a regex, see
.B SELECTING OBJECTS
in
.BR otpme (1).
.TP
\fBshow_oid\fR [\fB\-\-full\fR] \fIgroup\fR
Show the OID of the group: \fB\%group|<realm>/<name>\fR.
With \fB\-\-full\fR the full OID, which names the site and unit as well: \fB\%group|<realm>/<site>/<unit_path>/<name>\fR.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview_public:object\fR.
.TP
.B show_uuid \fIgroup\fR
Show the UUID of the group.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview_public:object\fR.
.TP
\fBlist\fR [\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-\-attribute\fR \fIattribute\fR] \fR[\fIpattern\fR]
List the group names, and with a pattern only those that match it, see
.B SELECTING OBJECTS
in
.BR otpme (1).
You get the groups you may change; \fB\-a\fR adds those you may only look at.
\fB\-\-attribute\fR matches the pattern against that attribute instead of the name.
.TP
\fBenable\fR [\fB\-\-share\-notify\fR|\fB\-\-no\-share\-notify\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-persist\-mount\fR] \fIgroup\fR
.TQ
\fBdisable\fR [\fB\-\-share\-notify\fR|\fB\-\-no\-share\-notify\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-persist\-mount\fR] \fIgroup\fR
Only an enabled group counts for the shares it is on: its hosts are hosts of a share that is limited to hosts.
Disabling it takes them away from there.
Logins and the group on the hosts stay as they are.
Tables show the status.
.br
Needs \fBenable:object\fR or \fBdisable:object\fR.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-share\-notify\fR, \fB\-\-no\-share\-notify
Whether the users of those shares are told, so that their hosts mount or unmount.
Without either the share decides (\fBshare_notifications\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-persist\-mount
Mount or unmount now, but leave the shares an offline login mounts as they are: after \fBenable\fR an offline login does not mount the share, after \fBdisable\fR it still does.
.RE
.TP
.B rename \fIgroup\fR \fInew_name\fR
Rename the group.
The new name must not be in use anywhere in the realm.
The groups the realm and the sites come with cannot be renamed.
.br
Needs \fBrename:object\fR.
.TP
\fBmove\fR [\fB\-\-keep\-acls\fR] \fIgroup\fR \fIunit\fR
Move the group into another unit.
The group keeps its ACLs; where ACL inheritance is on for it (off for groups by default, see \fBenable_acl_inheritance\fR), the default ACLs of the old unit are taken away and those of the new one given.
\fB\-\-keep\-acls\fR keeps all ACLs as they are.
.br
With a full path, \fI/<realm>/<site>/<unit>\fR, the group moves to that site; its policies go with it by name.
The groups the realm and the sites come with cannot be moved.
.br
Needs \fBmove\fR on the group and \fBadd:group\fR on the unit.
.TP
.B touch \fIgroup\fR
Write the index entries of the group again.
.br
Needs \fBtouch\fR.
.TP
.B auto_disable \fIgroup\fR \fItime\fR
Set auto\-disable time.
It is either a relative time with a leading \fB+\fR, e.g. \fB+30D\fR, or an absolute one, e.g. \fB"09:53 13.06.2023"\fR.
The units are \fBs\fR, \fBm\fR, \fBh\fR, \fBD\fR, \fBW\fR, \fBM\fR and \fBY\fR, see
.BR otpme (7).
A relative time counts from when it is set, and again from when the group is enabled.
\fB0\fR removes it.
It is refused for an object the realm or a site comes with, e.g. the admin user; a time set on one before does nothing.
.br
Needs \fBedit:auto_disable\fR.
.hy
.ad
.SS Token and Role Assignment
.na
.nh
A group's members are the owners of its tokens: on the hosts and in LDAP the group lists them (\fBmemberUid\fR).
A role in the group counts with its tokens, and with those of the roles it holds.
The users whose default group it is are members as well.
A disabled token or role counts for nothing.
.TP
\fBadd_token\fR [\fB\-\-sign\fR] [\fB\-\-tags\fR \fItag1,tag2\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-auto\-sign\fR] \fIgroup\fR \fItoken\fR
Put a token (\fI<user>/<token>\fR) into the group.
.br
It is signed when you ask for it, or when auto_sign is on: for you, or as config parameter of the group.
The signature is made with your login token and says the token is in this group.
A token signer of a host that wants this tag (\fBotpme\-tool add_signer \-\-tag group:<group>\fR) lets only the SSH keys of signed members in, see
.BR otpme\-tool (1).
.br
Needs \fBadd:token\fR.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-sign
Sign, where nothing above asked for it.
On a token that cannot be signed this is an error.
.TP
.B \-\-tags \fItag1,tag2\fR
Put more tags into the signature.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-auto\-sign
Do not sign this time.
.RE
.TP
\fBremove_token\fR [\fB\-\-keep\-sign\fR] \fIgroup\fR \fItoken\fR
Take the token out of the group.
The signatures made for this group go with it, each after a question; \fB\-\-keep\-sign\fR leaves them.
A token that is gone is taken out by its UUID.
.br
Needs \fBremove:token\fR.
.TP
\fBlist_tokens\fR [\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fItype\fR] [\fB\-\-token\-types\fR \fIt1,t2\fR] \fIgroup\fR
The tokens put into the group, disabled ones too; not those of its roles.
\fB\-\-return\-type\fR picks what is printed: \fBrel_path\fR (default, \fI<user>/<token>\fR), \fBread_oid\fR, \fBfull_oid\fR or \fBuuid\fR.
\fB\-\-token\-types\fR narrows it to those types, e.g. \fBhotp,totp\fR.
.br
Needs \fBview:tokens\fR.
.TP
.B add_role \fIgroup\fR \fIrole\fR
Put a role into the group.
One of another site is written as \fI<site>/<role>\fR.
.br
Needs \fBadd:role\fR.
.TP
.B remove_role \fIgroup\fR \fIrole\fR
Take the role out again.
A role that is gone is taken out by its UUID.
.br
Needs \fBremove:role\fR.
.TP
\fBlist_roles\fR [\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fItype\fR] \fIgroup\fR
The roles in the group, disabled ones too; one of another site as \fI<site>/<role>\fR.
\fB\-\-return\-type\fR picks what is printed: \fBname\fR (default), \fBread_oid\fR, \fBfull_oid\fR or \fBuuid\fR.
.br
Needs \fBview:roles\fR.
.hy
.ad
.SS User Management
.na
.nh
Who is a member of the group on the hosts is in its attribute \fBmemberUid\fR: \fBshow_ldif\fR \fIgroup\fR \fB\-a memberUid\fR, see \fBToken and Role Assignment\fR.
The two lists here are parts of it, and they list disabled ones too.
.TP
\fBlist_users\fR [\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fItype\fR] \fIgroup\fR
The users the tokens put into the group belong to; one user with three tokens is named once.
Not those that come through a role.
\fB\-\-return\-type\fR picks what is printed: \fBname\fR (default), \fBread_oid\fR, \fBfull_oid\fR or \fBuuid\fR.
.br
Needs \fBview:tokens\fR.
.TP
\fBlist_default_group_users\fR [\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fItype\fR] \fIgroup\fR
The users whose default group this is.
\fB\-\-return\-type\fR as above.
.br
Needs \fBview:default_group_users\fR.
.hy
.ad
.SS Host Management
.na
.nh
The hosts of a group count for one thing: on a share that is limited to hosts and has the group (\fBotpme\-share add_group\fR), they are hosts of the share, as long as the group is enabled.
Logins and the group on the hosts do not depend on them.
.TP
\fBadd_host\fR [\fB\-\-share\-notify\fR|\fB\-\-no\-share\-notify\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-persist\-mount\fR] \fIgroup\fR \fIhost\fR
Put a host into the group.
Host names are unique in the realm, so the host is given by its name alone, whatever site it is in.
A node cannot be put in.
.br
Needs \fBadd:host\fR.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-share\-notify\fR, \fB\-\-no\-share\-notify
Whether the users of those shares are told, so that the host mounts the share.
Without either the share decides (\fBshare_notifications\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-persist\-mount
Mount now, but an offline login does not mount the share.
.RE
.TP
\fBremove_host\fR [\fB\-\-share\-notify\fR|\fB\-\-no\-share\-notify\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-persist\-mount\fR] \fIgroup\fR \fIhost\fR
Take the host out again.
On the shares it loses, it is told to unmount, unless it is still on the share some other way.
A host that is gone is taken out by its UUID.
.br
Needs \fBremove:host\fR.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-share\-notify\fR, \fB\-\-no\-share\-notify
As above, for the unmount.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-persist\-mount
Unmount now, but an offline login still mounts the share.
.RE
.TP
\fBlist_hosts\fR [\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fItype\fR] \fIgroup\fR
The hosts in the group, disabled ones too.
\fB\-\-return\-type\fR picks what is printed: \fBname\fR (default), \fBread_oid\fR, \fBfull_oid\fR or \fBuuid\fR.
.br
Needs \fBview:hosts\fR.
.hy
.ad
.SS Sync User Management
.na
.nh
A sync user is a user whose account is copied to a host without any token of theirs going with it.
A group passes its sync users on to the hosts that name it as sync group (\fBotpme\-host add_sync_group\fR, with \fBenable_sync_groups\fR), together with the sync users of its roles and of the roles inside them.
A sync user is no member of the group on the hosts.
.TP
.B add_sync_user \fIgroup\fR \fIuser\fR
Have that user's account copied to those hosts.
The user may be of any site.
.br
Needs \fBadd:sync_user\fR.
.TP
.B remove_sync_user \fIgroup\fR \fIuser\fR
Stop that.
A user who is gone is taken out by the UUID.
.br
Needs \fBremove:sync_user\fR.
.TP
\fBlist_sync_users\fR [\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fItype\fR] \fIgroup\fR
The sync users of the group, disabled ones too; not those of its roles.
\fB\-\-return\-type\fR picks what is printed: \fBname\fR (default), \fBread_oid\fR, \fBfull_oid\fR or \fBuuid\fR.
.br
Needs \fBview:sync_users\fR.
.hy
.ad
.SS Object Changelog
Every change to the object writes an entry: who made it, when, and what was done.
A change can carry a comment of its own, through the global option \fB\-\-changelog\fR.
.PP
Unless the \fBchangelog\fR config parameter says otherwise.
It is read along the cascade (group, unit, site), and nothing is written where it is off.
Without any value anywhere it is on.
.PP
An \fIentry_id\fR is the number the listing shows in front of an entry, counted from 0.
.PP
Only the comment of an entry can be changed or removed.
What was done and by whom stays as it is.
.PP
A command that changes something asks first; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
.B changelog \fIgroup\fR
Show the changelog of the group, one entry per line: its \fIentry_id\fR, the time, who made the change and what was done, then a detail in round brackets and the comment in square brackets where there are any.
Needs the ACL \fBview:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B edit_changelog \fIgroup\fR \fIentry_id\fR \fIcomment\fR
Set the comment of that entry, or replace the one it has.
That writes an entry of its own.
Needs the ACL \fBedit:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B del_changelog \fIgroup\fR \fIentry_id\fR
Remove the comment of that entry.
The entry itself stays, and an entry without a comment is refused.
That writes an entry of its own.
Needs the ACL \fBedit:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B clear_changelog \fIgroup\fR
Remove every entry of the changelog.
Afterwards it holds one entry: that it was cleared.
.br
This needs more than the ACL \fBclear:changelog\fR: the config parameter
.B allow_changelog_clearing
has to allow it (see \fBotpme\fR(7)).
It is true by default, but where no value is found at all, clearing is refused.
A value set on the token you are signed in with counts first.
Without one, the value of the group counts, read along its cascade.
.SS Policy Management
A \fIpolicy\fR is the name of a policy, not a path.
It is looked for in the site of the group.
Which policies there are, and what each of them does, is in
.BR otpme\-policy (1).
.PP
Two policy types fit a group: \fBauthonaction\fR and \fBautodisable\fR.
.PP
Attaching and detaching ask before they change anything; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
.B add_policy \fIgroup\fR \fIpolicy\fR
Attach a policy to the group.
.br
A policy has a list of object types it is for; one that does not name \fBgroup\fR is refused.
So is a second policy of a type that allows only one per object, even when the first one is disabled, and a policy the group already has.
.br
An auto-disable policy with \fB\-u\fR is refused, because a group does not keep its last use.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBassign:policy\fR.
.TP
.B remove_policy \fIgroup\fR \fIpolicy\fR
Remove a policy from the group.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBremove:policy\fR, and that one verbatim: a plain \fBremove\fR does not cover it.
Detaching a policy takes away what it enforces, so it must not be cheaper than attaching one.
.TP
\fBlist_policies\fR [\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fITYPE\fR] [\fB\-\-policy\-types\fR \fIt1,t2\fR] \fIgroup\fR
List the policies attached to the group, the disabled ones too, marked \fB(disabled)\fR.
Use \fB\-\-return\-type\fR to select the attribute returned (\fBname\fR, \fBread_oid\fR, \fBfull_oid\fR, \fBuuid\fR); without it the names.
Only the names are marked.
Use \fB\-\-policy\-types\fR to list only policies of the given types; these are policy types, not policy names.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:policies\fR.
.SS ACL Management
An ACL says what somebody may do with this group.
The somebody is a role or a token, never a user.
.PP
What the three forms \fBacl\fR, \fB+acl\fR and \fB++acl\fR mean is under \fBACLs\fR in
.BR otpme (7).
Tab completion after \fBadd_acl\fR offers the ACLs a group knows.
A group has no default ACLs, \fB+acl\fR and \fB++acl\fR: nothing lies in a group that could get them.
.PP
Adding and removing ask first; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
.B add_acl \fIgroup\fR \fIowner_type\fR \fIowner\fR \fIacl\fR[:\fIvalue\fR][,\fIacl\fR[:\fIvalue\fR]]
Give a role or a token one or more ACLs on this group.
.br
\fIowner_type\fR is \fBrole\fR or \fBtoken\fR.
\fIowner\fR names it the way \fBlist_acls\fR prints it: a role as \fIsite/name\fR, or by its name alone on its own site; a token as \fI\%user/token\fR.
An ACL can carry a value, for example \fBview:tokens\fR.
To give several ACLs at once, separate them with commas: \fB\%view:tokens,add:token\fR.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBadd:acl\fR.
.TP
.B del_acl \fIgroup\fR \fIacl\fR[,\fIacl\fR]
Take those ACLs away again.
.br
Here an \fIacl\fR is the whole entry, owner and all, the way \fBlist_acls\fR prints it: \fB\%role:berlin/helpdesk:view:tokens\fR.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBdelete:acl\fR.
.TP
.B list_acls \fIgroup\fR
Show the ACLs that lie on this group, i.e. who may do what with it.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:acl\fR.
.TP
.B enable_acl_inheritance \fIgroup\fR
Let a move of the group swap its default ACLs: what the old unit handed down is taken away, and what the new unit hands down is given.
It does not by default; a move then leaves the ACLs of the group as they are.
.RS
.PP
Only a move asks this.
A new group gets the default ACLs of its unit, and \fBadd_acl \-a\fR or \fB\-r\fR on a unit reach it either way.
A move with \fB\-\-keep\-acls\fR leaves its ACLs alone all the same.
.PP
A move by somebody who does not hold \fBadd:acl\fR and \fBdelete:acl\fR on the group leaves its ACLs alone as well, and says so.
Otherwise moving an object into a unit would be a way of handing yourself the default ACLs of that unit.
.PP
Groups had it on until config version 2, which switches it off for all of them (see \fBupgrade\fR in
.BR otpme\-site (1)).
.PP
Needs the ACL \fBenable:acl_inheritance\fR.
.RE
.TP
.B disable_acl_inheritance \fIgroup\fR
A move leaves the ACLs of the group as they are again.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBdisable:acl_inheritance\fR.
.SS Group Configuration
.na
.nh
A group has three config parameters: \fBchangelog\fR, \fBallow_changelog_clearing\fR and \fBauto_sign\fR, see \fBCONFIG PARAMETERS\fR.
A parameter is looked up at the group, then at each unit above it, then at the site.
.TP
\fBconfig\fR [\fB\-d\fR] [\fB\-a\fR] \fIgroup\fR \fIparameter\fR \fR[\fIvalue\fR]
Set a parameter on the group.
.RS
.TP
.B \-d
Remove the parameter from the group, or with a value only that one value from a list.
It is not set back to a default: what applies afterwards is what a unit above or the site says.
.TP
.B \-a
Append the value to a parameter that holds a list, instead of replacing what is there.
.RE
.IP
The ACL is per parameter, \fBset:config:<parameter>\fR.
.TP
.B show_config \fIgroup\fR \fR[\fIparameter\fR]
The parameters set on the group itself, or only the one named.
Nothing is shown for a parameter that the group gets from a unit or from the site.
.TP
.B get_config \fIgroup\fR \fIparameter\fR
What the parameter comes to for the group, once the group, the units above and the site have been looked at.
So this answers what applies, where \fBshow_config\fR answers what was set here.
.TP
.B description \fIgroup\fR \fR[\fIdescription\fR]
Set the description.
Without one OTPme asks and offers the current text for editing.
Needs \fBedit:description\fR.
.TP
\fBinfo\fR [\fB\-\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] \fIgroup\fR \fR[\fIinfo\fR]
Set free-form info text.
If \fIinfo\fR is omitted, the current info text is opened in the editor named by the \fBEDITOR\fR environment variable.
Needs \fBedit:info\fR.
.br
Without \fB\-\-language\fR the text is the administrator's own note.
Nothing shows the info of a group to a user, so a text for a language stays a note as well.
.TP
\fBdump_info\fR [\fB\-\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] \fIgroup\fR
Write one of those texts to stdout.
\fB\-\-language\fR picks which one; without it you get the note of the administrator.
Needs \fBview:info\fR.
.TP
\fBshow_info\fR \fIgroup\fR
List the languages the group has a text for, one per line, with the first line of the text.
The note of the administrator comes first, as \fB\-\fR.
An empty text is not listed.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:info\fR.
.hy
.ad
.SS Extensions and LDAP Attributes
A group is also an LDAP entry, \fBcn=\f(BIgroup\fR under its unit, for example \fBcn=developers,ou=groups,ou=berlin,dc=example,dc=org\fR.
An extension is what gives it one: the object classes it is of, and the attributes those classes allow.
What \fBldapd\fR serves and what \fBshow_ldif\fR prints comes from them.
On a host, the members of a group are in the NSS files as well.
.PP
A group has the extensions \fBbase\fR and \fBposix\fR.
\fBposix\fR makes it a \fBposixGroup\fR with \fBcn\fR, \fBgidNumber\fR and \fBmemberUid\fR, and it needs \fBbase\fR.
Neither can be removed: \fBposix\fR brings \fBcn\fR, which names the entry, and \fBposix\fR needs \fBbase\fR.
No other extension is for groups.
.PP
OTPme fills in two attributes itself:
.RS
.TP
.B gidNumber
Taken from the ID range policy of the unit or the site when the group is added, see
.BR otpme\-policy (1).
A number you set yourself must be in that range and must not be used by another group.
When it changes, the users with this group as their primary group get the new number as well.
.TP
.B memberUid
The users of the group: the owners of its tokens, also of the tokens in its roles, and the users with this group as their primary group.
OTPme writes it when the members change, so it is read-only.
\fBshow_ldif \-a memberUid\fR lists the members.
.RE
.PP
The commands that change something ask first; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
.B add_extension \fIgroup\fR \fIextension\fR
Add an extension to the group.
One that is not for groups is refused, and so is one the group already has; for a group, that is every extension today.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBadd:extension\fR.
.TP
.B remove_extension \fIgroup\fR \fIextension\fR
Take it away again, with the classes and attributes it brought.
For a group, both extensions stay, see above.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBremove:extension\fR.
.TP
.B add_attribute \fIgroup\fR \fIattribute\fR\fB=\fR[\fIvalue\fR]
Set an LDAP attribute of the group.
.RS
.PP
Only an attribute \fBposixGroup\fR allows.
\fBcn\fR is read-only: it is the name of the group.
\fBmemberUid\fR is read-only as well, see above.
\fBuserPassword\fR is refused: a group is no account to log in with.
An attribute can hold more than one value, so this adds one and leaves the others.
.PP
Everything after the first \fB=\fR is the value.
Without a value OTPme fills in its own where it has one: for \fBdescription\fR, the description of the group.
.PP
The ACL is per attribute, \fBadd:attribute:\f(BIattribute\fR, and a plain \fBadd:attribute\fR covers all of them.
.RE
.TP
.B modify_attribute \fIgroup\fR \fIattribute\fR \fIold_value\fR \fInew_value\fR
Put \fInew_value\fR in the place of \fIold_value\fR, and leave the other values of the attribute alone.
An \fIold_value\fR the group does not have is an error.
This is the way to give a group another \fBgidNumber\fR.
.br
The ACL is \fBedit:attribute:\f(BIattribute\fR.
.TP
.B del_attribute \fIgroup\fR \fIattribute\fR\fB=\fR[\fIvalue\fR]
Remove that value, or the whole attribute where no value is given.
\fBcn\fR and \fBmemberUid\fR cannot be removed.
.br
The ACL is \fBdelete:attribute:\f(BIattribute\fR.
.TP
.B add_object_class \fIgroup\fR \fIclass\fR
Add an LDAP object class.
Only a class an extension of the group knows is taken, and for a group that is \fBposixGroup\fR, which it has already.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBadd:object_class\fR.
.TP
.B del_object_class \fIgroup\fR \fIclass\fR
Remove an object class again, with the attributes only it allows.
The class that allows the attribute naming the entry stays, and for a group that is \fBposixGroup\fR, its only class.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBdelete:object_class\fR.
.TP
\fBshow_ldif \f(BIgroup\fR [\fB\-a\fR \fIattribute1,attribute2\fR]
The group as LDAP sees it.
.B \-a
prints only those attributes.
.SS Export and Cleanup
.TP
\fBexport\fR [\fB\-\-password\fR \fIpassword\fR] \fIgroup\fR
Write the whole object of the group to stdout, as JSON: its tokens, roles, ACLs, policies, config parameters, LDAP attributes and the rest.
\fBotpme\-tool import\fR reads it back.
.br
A group holds no key and no secret, so \fB\-\-password\fR has nothing to encrypt: the output is in clear either way.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBexport\fR.
.TP
.B remove_orphans \fIgroup\fR
Remove references to objects that are gone: tokens and roles of the group that were deleted, orphan ACLs and policies.
.br
It asks once for what it found; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBremove:orphans\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
Every option belongs to one command and is described with that command above.
.PP
Global options work with every command, see
.BR otpme (1).
.SH CONFIG PARAMETERS
A group takes these.
What each of them does, and how a parameter is resolved, is in
.BR otpme (7).
.TP
.B auto_sign \fR(bool, default: false)
Whether \fBadd_token\fR signs the token it puts into the group, as \fB\-\-sign\fR does, see above.
One set on your login token itself comes first and counts for everything you change.
\fBenable_autosign\fR of your user signs as well, see
.BR otpme\-user (1).
\fB\-\-no\-auto\-sign\fR skips it for one call.
.TP
.B changelog \fR(bool, default: true)
Whether the commands on this group are written to its changelog.
.TP
.B allow_changelog_clearing \fR(bool, default: true)
Whether that changelog may be cleared.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS A group for the developers
.TP
.B otpme-group add developers
The group goes into the unit \fBgroups\fR and gets the next free \fBgidNumber\fR.
.TP
.B otpme-group add_token developers alice/login
\fBalice\fR is a member: the name is in \fBmemberUid\fR, on the hosts as well.
.TP
.B otpme-group add_role developers devops
Everybody with a token in the role \fBdevops\fR is a member too.
.TP
.B otpme-group list_users developers
The users whose tokens are in the group; not those that come through a role.
.TP
.B otpme-group list_default_group_users developers
Who has it as primary group.
.SS A group with a fixed GID
.TP
.B otpme-group add \-\-attributes gidNumber=5000 staff
The number must be in the ID range of the unit or the site.
.TP
.B otpme-group modify_attribute staff gidNumber 5000 5001
A new number; the users with \fBstaff\fR as primary group get it as well.
.SS SSH only for signed members
.TP
.B otpme-group add_token \-\-sign developers alice/ssh
The token of \fBalice\fR is signed for the group.
.TP
.B otpme-tool add_signer \-\-signer\-type token \-\-tag group:developers 'role|example.org/berlin/admins'
On a host: an SSH key is let in only with a signature of an admin that carries the tag of the group, see
.BR otpme\-tool (1).
.SS On a host
.TP
.B otpme-group show_ldif developers \-a memberUid
The members as LDAP sees them.
.TP
.B getent group developers
The same through NSS on a host.
